INT. TOLL PLAZA - BOOTH 4 - NIGHT

A glass cubicle on an empty highway. A space heater ticks. A thermos, a paperback, a laminated sign reading EXACT CHANGE.

WEN DAI (50s, reading glasses on a beaded chain) marks her page and slides the window open as headlights arrive.

PRAKASH NAIR (30s, rumpled suit, a sleeping toddler visible in the car seat behind him) hands her a twenty. She makes change slowly, counting it out coin by coin.

WEN
Long drive?

PRAKASH
Not too bad.

His hands stay on the wheel at ten and two. She hands him the coins. He puts them in the cup holder one at a time.

A phone rings in the booth. Wen picks it up.

MRS. OKAFOR (V.O.)
(through the receiver, tinny)
Wen, it's Ngozi at the state barracks. Is a grey Corolla at your gate?

Wen looks at the grey Corolla. She looks at the toddler. She looks at Prakash's hands.

WEN
(into phone)
No. Quiet night. I'll call you back.

She hangs up. Prakash has not moved.

WEN (CONT'D)
Your tail light is out. Take the service road. The patrol doesn't run it.

He stares at her. She slides the window shut and opens her paperback.
